Yes, it is possible to fake the entire WebSocket communication without a real server.

To do this, use `page.routeWebSocket()` (or `browserContext.routeWebSocket()`). By default, when you route a WebSocket, Playwright does not connect to the actual server, allowing you to mock the entire communication. You should call this method before navigating the page.

To mock the communication, use the `ws.onMessage()` method on the `WebSocketRoute` object to listen for messages from the page and `ws.send()` to respond to them.

**Example:**

```javascript
await page.routeWebSocket('wss://example.com/ws', ws => {
  ws.onMessage(message => {
    if (message === 'request') {
      ws.send('response');
    }
  });
});
```

Because you do not call `webSocketRoute.connectToServer()` inside the handler, Playwright assumes the WebSocket is being mocked and opens it inside the page automatically.

